 Scenario: Automated Environment Provisioning Using Terraform
🛠 Business Problem:
Our company needed a way to quickly and consistently spin up development, staging, and production environments for our web application.

Manually provisioning environments led to inconsistencies.
Developers often faced configuration drift between environments.
Scaling and resource optimization were time-consuming without automation.
✅ Solution:
We used Terraform to automate the infrastructure provisioning process.

Created reusable Terraform modules for environments.
Used Terraform Workspaces to manage dev, staging, and prod.
Ensured consistent configurations across all environments.
🚀 Terraform Implementation
1️⃣ Define a Reusable Terraform Module for EC2 Instances
We created a Terraform module to provision an EC2 instance with required security groups, IAM roles, and a predefined AMI.

h
Copy
Edit
module "ec2_instance" {
  source          = "./modules/ec2"
  instance_type   = var.instance_type
  ami_id         = var.ami_id
  environment    = var.environment
}
2️⃣ Use Terraform Workspaces for Different Environments
Terraform Workspaces allow us to manage multiple environments without duplicating code.

sh
Copy
Edit
# Create separate environments
terraform workspace new dev
terraform workspace new staging
terraform workspace new production

# Switch between environments
terraform workspace select dev
3️⃣ Variables for Environment-Specific Configurations
Each environment (dev, staging, prod) has different instance sizes and database settings.

hcl
Copy
Edit
variable "instance_type" {
  type    = string
  default = "t2.micro"
}

variable "environment" {
  type    = string
  default = "dev"
}
4️⃣ Provision Load Balancer, Auto Scaling, and RDS
Each environment had an ALB, Auto Scaling Group (ASG), and RDS Database configured via Terraform.

hcl
Copy
Edit
resource "aws_autoscaling_group" "asg" {
  desired_capacity     = var.desired_capacity
  min_size            = 1
  max_size            = 5
  launch_template {
    id      = aws_launch_template.web.id
    version = "$Latest"
  }
}

resource "aws_rds_instance" "database" {
  engine         = "mysql"
  instance_class = var.db_instance
  allocated_storage = 20
  username       = "admin"
  password       = "securepassword"
  publicly_accessible = false
}
